{
  "unit_id": "A1-01",
  "level": "A1",
  "title": "Nice to Meet You!",
  "grammar_topic": "Verb to be: afirmativo, negativo y preguntas",
  "vocab_theme": "Saludos, presentaciones, información personal",
  "can_do": "Puede saludar, presentarse, decir quién es y de dónde es",

  "grammar_explanation": {
    "language": "es",
    "core_rule": "El verbo 'to be' (ser/estar) cambia según la persona: am, is, are. En español muchas veces no se necesita un verbo para presentarse ('Yo, Camila'), pero en inglés el verbo 'to be' SIEMPRE tiene que aparecer.",
    "structure_notes": [
      { "label": "I", "explanation": "am / am not / Am I...?", "example": "I am Camila. I'm not a teacher. Am I late?" },
      { "label": "You", "explanation": "are / aren't / Are you...?", "example": "You are a student. You aren't from here. Are you ready?" },
      { "label": "He / She / It", "explanation": "is / isn't / Is he...?", "example": "She is a teacher. He isn't from Cartagena. Is it far?" },
      { "label": "We / You / They", "explanation": "are / aren't / Are we...?", "example": "We are friends. They aren't students. Are we late?" }
    ],
    "examples": [
      "I am Camila. (Soy Camila.)",
      "She is a teacher. (Ella es profesora.)",
      "They are not from Bogotá. (Ellos no son de Bogotá.)",
      "Are you a student? (¿Eres estudiante?)"
    ],
    "common_mistake": "El error más común de hispanohablantes es OMITIR el verbo 'to be' ('Yo profesora' en vez de 'I am a teacher'). En inglés el verbo siempre va."
  },

